\ Test that a compiled recursion in a task grows the task's stack instead of overflowing at its
\ base. Compiled code checks its own copy of the stack limit, so the growth has to reach that copy.
\
\ The budget alone cannot show growth, because a task reports the budget it can grow to from the
\ start. So the test compares bytes per level, the budget divided by the depth reached, against the
\ main task's. A task that grows reaches its whole budget, and the two figures are close. A task
\ that overflowed at its base would reach about a twentieth of its budget, and its figure would be
\ about twenty times the main task's.

use "testing" ;

deep: stack-recursive ( n -- n ) [ dup 0 > [ 1 - deep 1 + ] [ ] if ] ;

\\ Whether `deep` reaches `n` levels without the guard firing.
reaches?: ( n: fixnum -- n ? ) [ [ dup deep drop t ] [ drop f ] recover ] ;

\\ The deepest power of two `deep` reaches. It is within a factor of two of the real depth.
reachable-depth: ( -- depth ) [ 1 [ reaches? ] [ 2 * ] while 2 / ] ;

\\ Bytes of budget per level reached on the stack the caller runs on.
bytes-per-level: ( -- n ) [ stack-budget? reachable-depth / ] ;

"overflow in a task" test-section

[
  [ [ 1000000000 deep drop ok: ] [ error-type: @get ] recover ] spawn await
  stack-overflow: "a compiled recursion in a task still overflows" assert=
] task-scope

"growth in a task" test-section

\ One statement, so the main task's figure, which depends on the host's frame size, is never shown.
bytes-per-level [
  4 * [ bytes-per-level ] spawn await >
  "a compiled recursion in a task reaches its budget, as the main task does" assert-true
] curry task-scope
